\title{The Additive Uncertainty Principle on Cyclic Groups of Prime Order}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $p$ be a prime and let $f : \Zp \to \CC$ be nonzero, with discrete Fourier transform
$\hat f(k) = \sum_x e^{-2\pi i kx/p} f(x)$.  We give a complete, self-contained development of the
\emph{additive} uncertainty principle $|\supp f| + |\supp \hat f| \ge p+1$, strictly stronger than
the classical multiplicative bound $|\supp f|\cdot|\supp\hat f|\ge p$ valid for every modulus.  The
engine is Chebotarev's theorem: for prime $p$ every square submatrix of the Fourier matrix
$(\zeta^{xy})$ is nonsingular.  We present Frenkel's elementary proof in full, based on the order of
vanishing at $1$ of the integer polynomial $\det(X^{a_ib_j})$ and on the identity
$\big(\prod_{j<k}j!\big)G_N = V(a)V(b)$ identifying its critical coefficient as a product of two
Vandermonde determinants.  We then prove: an exact converse (every pair of sets $A,B$ with
$|A|+|B| = p+1$ occurs as the support pair of some signal); a primality criterion (for $n\ge2$ the
additive bound holds for all nonzero $f$ on $\Zn$ if and only if $n$ is prime, with subgroup
indicators as counterexamples); deterministic sparse recovery ($k$-sparse signals are determined by
their spectrum on an arbitrary set of $2k$ frequencies, and $2k-1$ never suffice, for any sampling
pattern); a Fourier interpolation theorem and the statement that every rectangular block of the
prime Fourier matrix has rank $\min(|A|,|B|)$; and a zero-counting theorem asserting that $\hat f$
vanishes at strictly fewer than $|\supp f|$ frequencies.
\end{abstract}

